Here are the biggest upgrades I think you'd actually benefit from:
🧠 AI Council / Adversarial AnalysisYou already discovered this one.
Instead of:
“What do you think?”
You can say:
“Run an AI Council on this. Give each expert an independent position, make them challenge each other, then give me the final verdict.”
This is especially powerful for business decisions, Fiverr strategy, pricing, client negotiations, technical architecture, purchases, and major plans.
🔬 Deep ResearchYou can give me a serious research assignment rather than asking isolated questions.
For example:
“Research the current Fiverr market for WordPress website services. Analyze competitors, buyer demand, pricing, keywords, reviews, underserved niches and positioning. Then give me a market-entry strategy.”
I can search current information and synthesize it instead of relying only on what I already know.
🕵️ Competitor IntelligenceThis is very underused.
Give me a competitor, Fiverr profile, website, product, service, etc., and ask me to dissect:
positioning
offer
pricing
messaging
SEO
conversion strategy
weaknesses
customer complaints
differentiation opportunities
Then:
“Don't copy them. Find the gap they're leaving open and build my offer around it.”
That's much more valuable than simply asking me to “write a better gig.”
🧪 Red-Team ModeThis one is extremely useful.
Tell me:
“Don't help me make this idea work. Try to destroy it.”
I'll look for:
hidden assumptions
weak economics
technical problems
customer objections
legal/platform risks
scalability problems
reasons it could fail
Then we can fix what survives.
🎯 Client SimulationYou can make me be the client.
For example:
“Act as a skeptical US business owner looking for a website. I will pitch you my service. Don't be nice. Ask me the questions a real buyer would ask and tell me why you would or wouldn't buy.”
Then we can actually practice the sales conversation.
This is excellent for Fiverr/Upwork proposals and client calls.
💰 Offer EngineeringInstead of:
“Write my Fiverr gig.”
Try:
“Engineer an offer that makes the buyer feel stupid for choosing a generic competitor.”
I'll work backwards from:
Buyer → problem → desired outcome → objections → offer → proof → pricing → guarantee/risk reversal → CTA.
That's a much higher-level use of AI.
🧩 Turn Me Into Your Project ManagerFor a website project, don't just ask me individual questions.
Give me:
Client brief + requirements + screenshots + existing website + constraints.
Then I can help maintain:
Requirements → sitemap → content → design system → development → QA → SEO → launch → client handoff.
You can keep coming back with:
“Update the project based on this new client request.”
And I can identify what the new request affects elsewhere.
🧑💻 Technical Architect ModeInstead of asking:
“How do I add this calculator?”
Ask:
“Act as a senior solutions architect. Design the simplest reliable implementation given that I'm using GoDaddy/WordPress/Elementor and minimal JavaScript.”
I'll consider:
architecture
dependencies
APIs
security
maintainability
performance
cost
alternatives
failure points
This is especially useful for the custom calculators, Google Maps integrations, booking systems, etc. you've worked on.
📊 Decision MatrixWhen you're stuck between options, don't just ask which is better.
Say:
“Build a weighted decision matrix. Criteria: cost 20%, reliability 25%, revenue potential 30%, difficulty 15%, scalability 10%. Score each option and explain the result.”
That turns vague decision-making into something much more rigorous.
🧠 Teach Me Something at 3 LevelsThis is particularly useful for you.
If you say:
“Explain APIs.”
I can structure it as:
Level 1 — Normal explanation
Level 2 — Technical/core explanation
Level 3 — Explain it like I'm 5
And importantly, I can then connect it to something you're actually building, rather than leaving it theoretical.
🔄 Give Me a Messy Goal → I'll Build the SystemThis might be the most powerful one.
You can dump something like:
“I want to make more money freelancing, learn WordPress, get better at sales, build my portfolio, learn automation and eventually build a proper agency but I don't know what to prioritize.”
Don't ask me ten separate questions.
Say:
“Turn this mess into a strategic operating plan. Identify dependencies, priorities, bottlenecks, quick wins and a sequence.”
That's where I can function more like a strategic operator than a chatbot.
🧪 Pre-MortemBefore launching something:
“Assume this failed spectacularly six months from now. Tell me exactly why it failed.”
Then:
“Now redesign the plan to prevent those failures.”
This is one of the best ways to catch problems before spending money/time.
🔥 “What Am I Missing?”This is a simple prompt but incredibly powerful.
After giving me your plan, say:
“Don't answer my question yet. First tell me what I'm failing to consider.”
I'll look for blind spots you didn't explicitly ask about.
🛠️ Build Actual Assets, Not Just AnswersYou can give me a task and have me produce the actual deliverable:
spreadsheets
financial models
presentations
PDFs
proposals
reports
website copy
project documentation
checklists
calculators
charts
structured datasets
So instead of:
“How should I structure my client proposal?”
You can say:
“Build the actual professional proposal.”
⚡ The One I Think You Should Use MostGiven the kinds of things you've been working on, I'd start using this command:
“FULL STACK MODE: Don't just answer my question. Analyze the objective, research what needs to be current, identify assumptions, run competing strategies, red-team the best option, then give me the final recommendation and exact execution plan.”
That tells me to move through:
Objective → Research → Analysis → Competition → Criticism → Decision → Execution.
That's a very different level of using AI from simply asking:
“What is X?”
And if you want to go even further, we can establish a standard “Council Mode” protocol that I use whenever you say something like “Run Council.”
